{
  "sticker": "flower",
  "skeleton": "magnolia flower on a short stem, facing the viewer, turned a little to the left: root at the stem's foot (on the ground); body (pivot at the stem's foot) carries the stem; leafL/leafR at the leaves' bases on the stem (wave); head at the top of the stem carries the flower, whose slot swaps between the cup as drawn, the cup opened wider and the closed bud; face on the flower's yellow centre with eyes, brows and mouth.",
  "sheets": [
    {
      "id": "parts",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a smiling magnolia flower: big cup-shaped cream and soft pink petals round a bumpy yellow centre with a face, on a short green stem with two glossy leaves. Draw this exact flower as a cut-out paper puppet: its separate parts laid out on one sheet, the same drawing style, colours, texture, line and proportions as in image 1, every part at the size it has in image 1.\n\nDraw each part on its own, spread out over the sheet with wide empty gaps between them, no part touching or overlapping another:\n1. the flower: the cup of cream and pink petals with its bumpy yellow centre, exactly as in image 1, with a completely blank face on the yellow centre — no eyes, no brows, no mouth — keep the rosy cheeks; without the stem.\n2. the flower opened wide in the sun: the same flower, the same size, its petals spread wider open around the yellow centre, which shows a little more, with the same blank face and rosy cheeks.\n3. the flower closed for the night: the same flower, its cream and pink petals folded up together into a smooth, closed, rounded-pointed bud, the yellow centre hidden inside; no face.\n4. the stem: the green stem alone, curved as in image 1, without leaves; its top end, where it tucks under the flower, a soft rounded end with no outline drawn across it.\n5. the left leaf: the glossy green leaf on the left of image 1, alone, with a rounded base with no outline drawn across it.\n6. the right leaf: the glossy green leaf on the right of image 1, alone, drawn for its own side, with a rounded base with no outline drawn across it.\n\nEvery part is drawn whole, including what is normally hidden behind another part. No text, no labels, no numbers, no outline around the parts beyond the drawing's own line, no shadows, no ground, no grass, no flying petals or sparkles. Fully transparent background."
    },
    {
      "id": "face",
      "size": "1536x1024",
      "references": [
        "parts"
      ],
      "prompt": "Image 1 is a sticker of a smiling magnolia flower with a small happy face on its bumpy yellow centre; image 2 shows the same flower cut into puppet parts, with a blank face. Draw the flower's face pieces as separate stickers on one sheet: the same drawing style, colours and line as in image 1, a simple, small, sweet cartoon face, every piece at the size it has on the flower in image 1, so it fits the blank face in image 2. In image 1 both eyes look to the left: each dark-brown iris sits towards the left of its eye, the white on its right, a white sparkle at its top.\n\nDraw each piece on its own, upright, in four tidy rows with wide empty gaps between them, nothing touching:\nRow 1, the eye on the LEFT of image 1, five times:\n1. as it is in image 1: a small oval glossy dark-brown eye with a white sparkle, looking to the left.\n2. wide open and surprised: bigger and rounder, looking the same way, with its white sparkle.\n3. gently closed in sleep: a soft downward-curving line, as wide as the open eye.\n4. closed in a happy smile: an upward-curving arc, like an upside-down U, with two tiny lashes at its outer (left) end.\n5. sad: open and round, a soft glossy brown eye with a white sparkle, looking a little down; no lid slanting across it.\nRow 2, the eye on the RIGHT of image 1, the same five in the same order, drawn for its own side, never a mirror of row 1; open, it looks the same way as row 1, to the left; happy, its lashes at its outer (right) end.\nRow 3: 11. the left eyebrow as in image 1, a tiny thin dark-brown arch. 12. the same left eyebrow worried: tilted up at its inner (right) end. 13. the right eyebrow as in image 1, a tiny thin arch. 14. the same right eyebrow worried: tilted up at its inner (left) end.\nRow 4, mouths, none with any teeth: 15. the mouth as it is in image 1: a small closed happy smile, a thin dark-brown curved line. 16. the mouth wide open in a big happy laugh, the inside red-pink with a little tongue. 17. the mouth open in a small round 'o', the inside pink. 18. the mouth in a small sad frown. 19. the mouth open in a big sleepy yawn, a tall oval, the inside pink.\n\nNo text, no labels, no numbers, no shadows, no face or head around the pieces. Fully transparent background."
    }
  ],
